 ## check.sh
 
-### §check-fails-open — the check never blocks a commit because of its own failure
-- Since: 2026-09-11
-- Decision: `set -uo pipefail` without `-e`. Every internal error still ends in `exit 0`; the script only warns.
-- Forbidden: `set -e` or `set -o errexit`, and any exit status other than 0.
-- Reason: with `set -e`, a `wc` failure on a staged-then-deleted file aborted the check and blocked every commit in
-  the repository for a day (2026-09-10). The check is advisory by design.
+### §check-fails-closed — a failing command aborts the check with a non-zero exit
+- Since: 2026-10-22 (maintainer override; supersedes §check-fails-open, 2026-09-11)
+- Decision: `set -euo pipefail`. Any command that fails aborts the check non-zero, which blocks the commit. Large
+  staged files are still only a warning: the script ends in `exit 0` once the scan has completed.
+- Forbidden: removing `-e`, or swallowing a command's failure (`|| true`, `2>/dev/null` on a step whose status matters).
+- Reason: the maintainer decided a broken check must surface and stop the commit, not pass silently.
+- Caveats, from the superseded record and the change:
+  - 2026-09-10: under an earlier `set -e`, a `wc` failure on a staged-then-deleted file blocked every commit for a
+    day. The `[ -f "$f" ] || continue` guard skips staged deletions; keep it.
+  - No bare `[ test ] && cmd` as the last command of the loop body. When the test is false it becomes the loop's
+    status, `pipefail` carries it into `big=$(…)`, and `-e` aborts. That would block every commit whose last staged
+    file is under the limit. Use `if …; then …; fi`.
+- Evidence: checked by hand on 2026-10-22. Small file only → 0; large file → warning, 0; staged deletion → 0;
+  unreadable staged file (`wc` fails) → 1; outside a git repository → 129.
